orm{x-\R}_{A}^2 =  rac{1}{2} 
orm{A\R - y}_{2}^2 +  rac{1}{2} \log \det A \R A^T$. Here, $\R$ is an arbitrary positive-definite matrix that serves as a parameter adjusting the regularization level, and $ 
orm{\cdot}_2 $ refers to the Euclidean (matrix-induced) norm. The properties of the proposed method have been investigated, which is known as the $\R$-optimal biconvex regularization technique (ROBERT). It was demonstrated that for the case where $A$ contains only entries in the interior of its convex domain is a global optimum of the proposed method. A numerical algorithm for implementing ROBERT on a computer has been developed, along with analysis in several important scenarios of matrices, including non-invertible and ill-posed cases. In addition to this theoretical analysis, ROBERT was validated against other techniques in a Monte Carlo study on the well-known multi-dimensional free-chip problem. ROBERT is open-source and available for use through the BitsAndTables package. A brief user guide for selecting parameters of ROBERT is also provided in the appendix. Furthermore, it was found that ROBERT is applicable in a wide range of real-world problems beyond underdetermined linear equation, including surface fitting, multi-dimensional free-chip problem, diffraction tomography, imaging in ROBERT, rank-reduced approximation, and community detection in graphs, the details of which are described in the appendix. Interestingly, it was observed that ROBERT is applicable in a wide range of scenarios with specific choices of the matrix $\R$ as a positive-definite matrix. In the case where the equation is non-invertible, ROBERT reduces to the well-known Tikhonov regularization technique if $\R$ is chosen as an identity matrix. A corresponding entry-level implementation of ROBERT for matrix inverse problems using MATLAB is provided in the appendix. The results of several numerical experiments indicate that
